                <pb facs="0341" n="197" xml:id="pg197"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00B/598: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-06-21T13:00:00+08:00">June 21, 1929—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:10 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>498. Your 195, June 14, 3 p.m., was repeated to the American Consul at Harbin who
                    has replied as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 18, 4 p.m. Recent arrivals and reports from Hailar and Manchuria
                            Station indicate that conditions normal on the border and that no
                            trouble even in Barga will occur this summer. Inclined to believe these
                            false rumors by Soviet agents in order to intimidate Chinese.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
